1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,352
Well, you've both been looking at different styles of managing individuals in companies and the workplace.

2
00:00:07,352 --> 00:00:09,650
How's the research going, Philip?

3
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:12,947
Well, I've been looking at why individualism,

4
00:00:12,947 --> 00:00:18,600
I mean individual differences, are such an important area of management studies.

5
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:24,756
When you think about any organization, be it a family business or a multinational company,

6
00:00:24,756 --> 00:00:28,860
they are all fundamentally a group of people working together.

7
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:34,520
But it's what these individuals contribute to their places of work that makes you realize how important they are.

8
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:37,100
Of course they bring different ideas

9
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,830
but it's also their attitudes and their experiences of learning.

10
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:43,800
Diversity is important in these areas too.

11
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,460
So why do people behave so differently from one another at work?

12
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:50,860
There are lots of reasons

13
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:54,890
but research has shown a lot of it comes down to personality.

14
00:00:54,890 --> 00:00:57,280
And the other factor is gender.

15
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:02,196
It's a well known fact that men and women do lots of things in different ways,

16
00:01:02,196 --> 00:01:04,040
and the workplace is no different.

17
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:08,050
Did you look at the effects of this variation on companies?

18
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:10,961
Yes, I did. On the positive side,

19
00:01:10,961 --> 00:01:14,720
exposure to such diversity helps encourage creativity

20
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:17,200
which is generally an asset to a company.

21
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:22,130
But unfortunately individual differences are also the root of conflict between staff

22
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:27,470
and they can lead to difficulties for management, which can sometimes be serious.

23
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:35,507
Thanks, Philip. So now I guess the two main things to remember here are to identify individual talent

24
00:01:35,507 --> 00:01:37,740
and then to utilize it.

25
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:43,370
So Janice, you were looking at identifying different talents in workers.

26
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:46,260
Do you think this is easy for managers to do?

27
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:50,199
Well, currently teamwork is in fashion in the workplace

28
00:01:50,199 --> 00:01:55,450
and in my opinion the importance of the individual is generally neglected.

29
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:58,588
What managers should be targeting is those employees

30
00:01:58,588 --> 00:02:02,100
who can take the lead in a situation

31
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:05,650
and are not afraid to accept the idea of responsibility.

32
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:15,700
That's true Janice but unfortunately many managers think the entire notion of encouraging individuality amongst their staff is far too hard.

33
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:17,509
Yes, that may be true

34
00:02:17,509 --> 00:02:25,466
but I think one of the most important tasks of managers is to consider the needs of the individual on one hand

35
00:02:25,466 --> 00:02:28,360
and group co-operation and conformity on the other.

36
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:33,000
It requires creative thinking on the part of management to avoid tension.

37
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,130
So Janice, what kind of people do you think companies should be looking for?

38
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:41,980
Well, it has to start from the very beginning

39
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:44,400
when companies are looking for new employees.

40
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:49,830
When the personnel department is choosing between applicants they need to look for someone

41
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:53,320
who's broken the mould and can think for themselves.

42
00:02:53,320 --> 00:03:00,920
Instead, people making these decisions often use a range of psychological tests to see

43
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:02,047
if a person is a problem solver,

44
00:03:02,047 --> 00:03:04,540
or will do as they're told.

45
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:09,000
I'm not convinced these qualities are actually the most important.

46
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,800
So do you think being a good team player is overrated?

47
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:18,617
NO, it's not overrated. You do need to learn the rules and learn them fast.

48
00:03:18,617 --> 00:03:23,270
No individual can get around this if you're working in an organization.

49
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:26,000
So how should managers deal with this?

50
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,594
Rewards. When an individual demonstrates the behaviour the organisation expects,

51
00:03:31,594 --> 00:03:35,510
some kind of incentive can be given.

52
00:03:35,510 --> 00:03:39,329
What's important here is that this happens right at the beginning

53
00:03:39,329 --> 00:03:42,800
so new recruits learn the rules of the system immediately.

54
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:47,580
Also the incentive should be something the individual actually wants

55
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:50,200
and this isn't always just money.

56
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:52,728
To come back to you, Philip.

57
00:03:52,728 --> 00:03:57,320
You were saying that recognition of good performers is essential

58
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:59,890
What else should managers be looking for?

59
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:05,882
Well, managing people means you not only have an understanding of your employees,

60
00:04:05,882 --> 00:04:10,030
but you also recognise the culture of the organization.

61
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:12,271
In fact, for some organizations creativity

62
00:04:12,271 --> 00:04:17,500
and individuality may be the last thing they want to see during working hours!

63
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:18,520
Very true.

64
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:22,420
Yes, but managing people isn't as easy as it looks.

65
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:26,560
For example, change in the workplace can be quite tricky

66
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:29,410
especially if there's a need to increase profit.

67
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:32,220
And at times like these

68
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:37,640
managers may have to give priority to profit rather than individual staff needs.

69
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:41,250
Yes, and that creates difficult situations for people.

70
00:04:41,250 --> 00:04:47,800
Yes but what's important is that managers are able to deal with quite high levels of personal stress.

71
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:53,327
During times of change they should be thinking not only about the strain on their staff

72
00:04:53,327 --> 00:04:56,090
but take time out to think of themselves.

73
00:04:56,090 --> 00:04:59,760
Absolutely. So what are the implications of that for...
